{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1360,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The sun rises in the east, the moon rises\nin the east, and the stars rise in the east..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5730,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but solar eclipses, oddly, come from the west,\nlike the April 2024 North American eclipse,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10360,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the August 2027 North African eclipse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12720,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except, not all total eclipses come from the\nwest - a few near the north and south poles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16830,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually head west for a bit before turning\naround and then heading east - all of which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20150,
    "dDurationMs": 1190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems very weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21340,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If eclipses are caused by the sun and the\nmoon, why don't they behave like the sun and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25250,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the moon?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26250,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key to the explanation is that the paths\nof the sun and moon through the sky depend"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29150,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the rotational speed of the objects involved,\nwhile the paths of eclipses depend on just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32790,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the plain old straight-line speed of the moon\nabove the earth's surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36020,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Viewed from the north pole, the earth and\nmoon both rotate counterclockwise - that is,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40760,
    "dDurationMs": 1010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards the east."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41770,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The path of the moon through the sky is determined\nby the line of sight from the earth's surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45540,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the moon, and because the earth is rotating\nfaster than the moon is orbiting, the sight"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49540,
    "dDurationMs": 4109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "line (and the moon) starts off pointing to\nthe east at moonrise, then as the earth rotates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53649,
    "dDurationMs": 3161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the moon appears to pass overhead and set\nin the west (even though the moon is traveling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56810,
    "dDurationMs": 1429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards the east the whole time)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58239,
    "dDurationMs": 4081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In contrast, the path of an eclipse is determined\nnot by the direction from us to the moon,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62320,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but by where the moon's shadow falls on the\nearth's surface, and the moon's shadow just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65760,
    "dDurationMs": 1390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "points away from the sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67150,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The moon is traveling \"eastwards\" around the\nearth at just over 2000 miles per hour, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70950,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its shadow travels at basically the same speed\n- eastwards at just over 2000 miles per hour."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75470,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The earth's surface is also moving to the\neast, but not nearly as quickly - the surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79150,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the equator is only moving around 1000\nmiles per hour, and slower the closer you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82440,
    "dDurationMs": 12050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get to the poles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94490,
    "dDurationMs": 3809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The moon's shadow easily outpaces the earth's\nsurface's eastward motion, which means eclipses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98299,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appear to move from west to east."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100159,
    "dDurationMs": 3791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Put another way, the face of the earth is\nabout 8000 miles across, so the moon (and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103950,
    "dDurationMs": 3959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its shadow) cross the earth in around 3 and\na half hours (and less near the poles), while"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107909,
    "dDurationMs": 3901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any point on the earth takes 12 hours to cross\nthe earth - it takes half a day to rotate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111810,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "halfway around the earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112810,
    "dDurationMs": 3769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's kind of weird that the moon can orbit\nslower than the earth rotates but travel faster;"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116579,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the moon has a long way to travel: nearly\none and a half million miles over its month-long"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120979,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit, which equates to around 2000 miles\nper hour."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123299,
    "dDurationMs": 4891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In contrast, a point on the equator only travels\n25 thousand miles each day, or around 1000"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128190,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "miles per hour."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129190,
    "dDurationMs": 3159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's no cosmic reason that eclipses on\nearth travel west to east - it's essentially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132349,
    "dDurationMs": 1051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a coincidence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133400,
    "dDurationMs": 2811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the earth were twice as big, or the moon\nwere half as far away (and therefore the circumference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136211,
    "dDurationMs": 2169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its orbit half as long), then the length\nof a month or day wouldn't change (and neither"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138380,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would the direction of moonrise), but the\nrelative linear speeds of the surface of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141440,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earth and the moon WOULD change, and eclipses\nmight move from east to west."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145010,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, if the earth and moon's sizes were\nadjusted so that the moon was traveling slower"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148580,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the earth's surface at noon but faster\nat other times of day, it would be possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151660,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for an eclipse to move east, then west, then\neast again... geometry is weird!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156600,
    "dDurationMs": 3711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Speaking of weird, those weird west-moving\neclipses near the poles happen because the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160311,
    "dDurationMs": 3598,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earth's axis of rotation is tilted, so it's\npossible for the moon's shadow to be moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163909,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the east but hitting part of the earth\non the \"night-time\" side of the planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167989,
    "dDurationMs": 3811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can get a rough idea by opening up google\nearth and drawing a bunch of straight west"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171800,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to east arrows across the earth to represent\neclipse paths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173870,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you look at these arrows from another vantage\npoint, oops suddenly the eclipse paths look"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177630,
    "dDurationMs": 2579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way more wonky, and some of them go \"backwards\"!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180209,
    "dDurationMs": 3081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And while we're in google earth, if you tilt\nthe earth like it is during the spring and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183290,
    "dDurationMs": 3479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fall and draw some horizontal lines, you can\nget a sense of why eclipse paths follow the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186769,
    "dDurationMs": 3741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curvy shapes they do (though actual eclipse\npaths are more complicated because the earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190510,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is also rotating at the same time)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192030,
    "dDurationMs": 3879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In summary, even though the moon orbits to\nthe east \"slower\" than the earth rotates,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195909,
    "dDurationMs": 3681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the sense that a month is longer than a\nday, the moon also orbits to the east \"faster\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199590,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the earth, in the sense that the moon\nis literally traveling at a faster eastward"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203190,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed than the surface of the earth - and\nthat's what determines the direction of an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206800,
    "dDurationMs": 29740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipse."
    } ]
  } ]
}
